{"attack":true,"case":"The target's central claim asserts the preference is defensible 'not as an inductive inference of future success, but as a methodological rule.' Yet the target's own transfer claim explicitly models the preference on an inductive inference: 'Just as we trust the organism that survived a plague to have some resistance, we trust the theory that survived falsification attempts to have some verisimilitude.' Inferring from plague-survival to resistance is paradigmatically inductive—it generalizes from past survival to a dispositional property expected to hold in future encounters. By mapping this directly onto the scientific case, the target uses an inductive analogy to argue the preference is non-inductive, which is self-undermining. Furthermore, the claim that the rule 'maximizes the potential for error detection' is a forward-looking predictive claim. The target provides no a priori argument for it, relying instead on the empirical biological analogy—an inductive source. Without such an argument, 'maximizes the potential for error detection' is either an inductive projection from past method-performance or an undefended assertion. In either case, the target's own evidentiary apparatus contradicts its stated anti-inductive conclusion.","premise":"The target claims the preference is non-inductive but supports it with an explicitly inductive analogy and a forward-looking predictive claim ('maximizes the potential for error detection') that lacks any a priori justification.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"8a266c8263dcbc547c60163bc19390765744b0f9a84bf9ab4bb5a103f1f9e62a","quote":"not as an inductive inference of future success, but as a methodological rule of 'critical rationality' that maximizes the potential for error detection (verisimilitude)"},{"block":"8a266c8263dcbc547c60163bc19390765744b0f9a84bf9ab4bb5a103f1f9e62a","quote":"Just as we trust the organism that survived a plague to have some resistance, we trust the theory that survived falsification attempts to have some verisimilitude"}],"counterexample":null,"successor_question":"Can the target reformulate its defense without relying on the biological-resistance analogy, or is every analogy it can draw upon inherently inductive—thereby exposing that the methodological rule itself requires an inductive foundation to be motivated?"}